{#
  THE TWO SITES SIDE BY SIDE.

  Takes `report` and `competitor`, both reportData records. Every figure here has already
  appeared on a sheet the reader has passed, with the table that shows its working — this
  page adds no measurement, it only puts two of them next to each other.

  NOTHING IS JUDGED. No winner, no arrows, no green and red: which way a difference cuts
  depends on what the business is for, and a capture cannot know that. A homepage that gives
  52% to routing is not better or worse than one that gives 18%, it is a different decision —
  and a report that picks a side is arguing a case it has no evidence for.
#}
{% import '_views/report/_macros.twig' as r %}

{% set mine = r.host(report.url) %}
{% set theirs = r.host(competitor.url) %}

{% set scores = [
    { label: 'Speed', a: report.speed ? report.speed.score / 10 : null, b: competitor.speed ? competitor.speed.score / 10 : null },
    { label: 'Technical', a: report.technical ? report.technical.score : null, b: competitor.technical ? competitor.technical.score : null },
    { label: 'Consistency', a: report.brand ? report.brand.score : null, b: competitor.brand ? competitor.brand.score : null }
] %}

{# Every segment either page spends anything on, so a category one of them has and the
   other does not still gets a row — an absence is a finding and must not be a gap. #}
{% set names = [] %}
{% for c in report.categories %}{% if c.share > 0 %}{% set names = names|merge([c.category]) %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% for c in competitor.categories %}{% if c.share > 0 and c.category not in names %}{% set names = names|merge([c.category]) %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}

<section class="r-sheet">
    <h1>Executive Comparison</h1>
    <p class="r-meta"><b>{{ mine }}</b> &bull; <b>{{ theirs }}</b></p>

    <h2>Scores</h2>
    <table class="r-speed">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>&nbsp;</th>
                <th class="r-n">{{ mine }}</th>
                <th class="r-n">{{ theirs }}</th>
                <th class="r-n">Change</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for s in scores %}
                <tr>
                    <td><b>{{ s.label }}</b></td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ s.a is null ? '—' : s.a|number_format(1) ~ '/10' }}</td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ s.b is null ? '—' : s.b|number_format(1) ~ '/10' }}</td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.change(s.a, s.b, 'points', 'up') }}</td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Segments</h2>
    <table class="r-speed">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Segment</th>
                <th class="r-n">{{ mine }}</th>
                <th class="r-n">{{ theirs }}</th>
                <th class="r-n">Change</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for name in names %}
                {% set a = report.categories|filter(c => c.category == name)|first %}
                {% set b = competitor.categories|filter(c => c.category == name)|first %}
                <tr>
                    <td><b>{{ name|title }}</b></td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(a ? a.share : 0) }}</td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(b ? b.share : 0) }}</td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.change(a ? a.share : 0, b ? b.share : 0, 'share') }}</td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    {# THE PAGE ITSELF, rather than what is on it. Neither of these has a better direction:
       a longer page is not a worse one, and ink is how much of the surface is painted rather
       than how much is said — a page of type reads as almost empty because letters are
       mostly the gaps between them. So the arrows are grey and the figures do the talking. #}
    <h2>Page</h2>
    <table class="r-speed">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Metric</th>
                <th class="r-n">{{ mine }}</th>
                <th class="r-n">{{ theirs }}</th>
                <th class="r-n">Change</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Page Length</b></td>
                <td class="r-n">{{ report.height|number_format }}px</td>
                <td class="r-n">{{ competitor.height|number_format }}px</td>
                <td class="r-n">{{ r.change(report.height, competitor.height) }}</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Ink to Surface</b></td>
                <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(report.coverage) }}</td>
                <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(competitor.coverage) }}</td>
                <td class="r-n">{{ r.change(report.coverage, competitor.coverage, 'share') }}</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    {# THE ONE THING THE TABLES CANNOT SAY. Every row above is two shares and a change; this
       is a share against an ABSENCE, judged against what each page is for. gov.je having no
       hero is the finding of this whole comparison and no row on this sheet states it.

       AND WHETHER THE COMPARISON IS FAIR AT ALL. If one page is a directory and the other a
       shop, 69% against 18% is not one beating the other, it is two different decisions —
       and a reader left to assume otherwise draws the wrong conclusion from a table this
       report handed them. See lib/expectations.mjs: when the purposes differ, the mismatch
       is stated and nothing is set against anything.

       Nothing at all when either page would not say what it is for. #}
    {% set reading = report.comparison|default(null) %}
    {% if reading and reading.worth %}
        <h2>Summary</h2>
        <div class="r-reading-block">
            <p>{{ reading.lead }}</p>
            {% if reading.points|length %}
                <ul class="r-reading">
                    {% for point in reading.points %}
                        <li>{{ point }}</li>
                    {% endfor %}
                </ul>
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</section>
